WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.720
None of the tombs were booby traps.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.750
There were false doors to some tombs,

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:14.430
a corridor designed to lead a
potential thief to a dead end.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:15.690
I'm Anthony Browder,

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:19.170
author of "Nile Valley
Contributions to Civilization"

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:21.600
and director of the ASA
Restoration Project,

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.530
currently excavating and
restoring three Kushite tombs.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.930
Today, we're going to be looking
at ancient Egyptian history

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.897
in film and determining how real they are.

00:00:39.122 --> 00:00:40.800
[Anthony laughs]

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.320
The top of a sarcophagus that size

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.840
would've weighed more than an automobile.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.970
All of the sarcophagi
that I've seen in Egypt

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:49.242
were made of stone.

00:00:49.242 --> 00:00:52.260
If you can imagine trying
to push an automobile

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.390
with two hands, it
would've been impossible.

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:57.420
The thieves who broke
into tombs such as these,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.400
there were typically several men,

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.640
and they used heavy-duty instruments.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.028
Where's the ushabti?

00:01:04.028 --> 00:01:05.211
Oh, well.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:09.120
There would be jewelry on the mummy,

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:10.620
as we saw in this instance.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:12.900
You would find that there were little caps

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.258
that were put on their fingers.

00:01:14.258 --> 00:01:16.008
I'm sorry, Mr. Great.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:21.720
They reference ushabti.
Ushabti are small figurines

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.500
of the departed soul that were supposedly

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.310
built to assist the dead in the afterlife.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:33.030
Ushabtis were never put inside
of the bodies of a mummy.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:36.060
They were typically buried around them,

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:39.120
outside of the sarcophagus,
and in some instances

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.060
there were boxes that contained ushabtis.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:46.385
The idea of someone

00:01:46.385 --> 00:01:49.176
going into a tomb and desecrating a tomb,

00:01:49.176 --> 00:01:51.240
as this character does,
is quite troubling.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.420
These are historical
artifacts that are priceless.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:56.970
There are hundreds of tombs in Egypt

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:59.220
that have been raided, similar to this.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.620
It's been happening
for thousands of years.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:05.033
I would rate it a 0 for
historical accuracy.

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:12.930
One of the things that I liked
about this particular film,

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:16.080
if you were to walk into
a well-preserved tomb,

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.800
the colors would be as
vibrant today as they were

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.500
when the tomb was created 3,000 years ago.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.500
One of the most spectacular tombs in Egypt

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.380
in the Valley of the Kings
is the tomb of Seti I.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.410
And the colors are so rich, so vibrant,

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:33.780
it looks as if it was
just painted recently.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.496
The images on the wall

00:02:35.496 --> 00:02:38.879
of a priest coming before Osiris,

00:02:38.879 --> 00:02:42.360
that is an image that comes
from the papyrus of Hunefer.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.970
So this is an accurate depiction

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:47.640
of a 19th-dynasty personality.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:50.670
It is a depiction that comes
straight from his papyrus,

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.070
and so I appreciate the
attention to detail.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:01.380
These glyphs depicted in this
scene are not accurate glyphs.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.710
The one symbol that is
accurate is the wavy line.

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.440
And then that's an easy
glyph to replicate.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.720
It's gibberish, but it looks good.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:11.253
It helps to sell the movie.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.013
I'm not aware of any of
the ancient Egyptians

00:03:22.013 --> 00:03:24.090
using a quartz crystal, if you will,

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:28.560
to illuminate a specific
portion of a temple or a tomb,

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.160
but what we do know is that
there were a number of temples

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.770
in ancient Egypt which
were oriented to the sun.

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:37.650
For example, at Karnak
Temple, there is an event

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.620
that takes place every winter
at the winter solstice:

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:43.440
A beam of sunlight shines down

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.930
the eastern part of the temple,

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:50.280
and it would illuminate this
room with a ray of light,

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.590
similar to what you see here in the film.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:54.810
I would give it a 10 for production value.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.060
The set designer gets a lot of credit

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:58.210
for doing their research.

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:06.720
What you saw in this battle scene

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:11.190
were what I consider to be
fairly accurate representations

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:15.090
of the chariots, of the
armor, and the weapons

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:19.320
that were used by the ancient
Egyptians and their enemies.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.500
There was a chariot
buried in King Tut's tomb.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:27.060
And King Tut lived about
100 years before Ramses,

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:28.893
who we see in this particular scene.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:35.040
There are many texts in Egypt

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.230
that describe battles such as this.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:41.040
The most famous archers
in the Egyptian army

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:42.930
were the Nubians. They were considered

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:45.150
the best archers in the Nile valley.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:47.375
Typically one would find

00:04:47.375 --> 00:04:50.491
on the outer pylon of temples

00:04:50.491 --> 00:04:54.990
images of the king in a
chariot going into battle

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:57.720
to defend Egypt against
the enemies of Egypt.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:00.660
This was, I guess, royal propaganda,

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:03.510
to show that the king was
the protector of the land.

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:06.660
Ridley Scott directed
this particular film.

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:09.930
He gives great attention to detail.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:12.333
I give him a 10 on this depiction.

00:05:13.641 --> 00:05:16.445
Ardeth: Thebes, home of Imhotep.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:21.273
Pharaoh's high priest, keeper of the dead.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:26.490
Well, actually, Imhotep
was a third-dynasty priest

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:30.540
to the King Djoser in Saqqara,
which is in northern Egypt.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:34.200
Imhotep was the historical
personality who was responsible

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.870
for creating the step pyramid of Saqqara.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:38.640
Imhotep is also considered

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:41.220
to be the world's first multigenius.

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:44.100
He was a physician, deified by the Greeks.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:47.097
He also was an architect and an engineer.

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:51.213
Ardeth: Birthplace of
Ankhesenamun, pharaoh's mistress.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:58.800
The reference to Ankhesenamun
as the mistress of Seti I

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.490
is an extreme case of someone
taking liberty with the truth.

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:06.415
Ankhesenamun was actually
the wife of King Tut,

00:06:06.415 --> 00:06:09.960
who was a 18th-dynasty personality,

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:13.320
and Seti I was a 19th-dynasty personality.

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:15.060
So these two historical figures,

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:17.250
and they are real historical figures,

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:20.070
but they lived about a
century apart from each other.

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:23.885
So I have no knowledge of Seti I

00:06:23.885 --> 00:06:26.243
having a mistress by the
name of Ankhesenamun.

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:36.997
He is depicted in this film
as having murdered the king.

00:06:36.997 --> 00:06:39.720
There is no historical evidence

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:42.360
of Imhotep murdering the king.

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:46.380
The representation of
Imhotep and the other priests

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:49.260
as being clean-shaven was accurate.

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:52.650
Typically, every priest
in Egypt was clean-shaven.

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:56.640
It was an act of cleanliness,
and it was an act of respect

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:59.846
for the positions that they
held within each temple.

00:06:59.846 --> 00:07:01.700
Ardeth: Ankhesenamun's soul had been sent

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:03.900
to the dark underworld,

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:05.760
her vital organs removed

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:08.940
and placed in five sacred canopic jars.

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:10.465
They made reference to,

00:07:10.465 --> 00:07:13.770
"her organs were placed
in five canopic jars."

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:15.000
Well, anyone who knows anything

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:16.500
about ancient Egyptian history knows

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:18.630
that there are only four canopic jars,

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:21.810
the stomach, the lungs, and
some of the other organs.

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:26.810
The removal of the organs
was to keep the body clean.

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:29.310
Ardeth: The worst of all ancient curses,

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:32.280
one so horrible had never
before been bestowed.

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:35.250
The reference to Imhotep's
tongue being cut out,

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.833
to my knowledge, that was not done.

00:07:37.833 --> 00:07:39.720
Ardeth: Imhotep's priests were condemned

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:41.550
to be mummified alive.

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:44.790
I'm not aware of any person
being mummified alive.

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:47.550
Mummification was a very time-consuming

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:51.000
and expensive process,
so there's no reason

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:53.610
why a person in the past
would've been buried alive.

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:56.190
And the process of
mummification in ancient Egypt

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:58.982
typically took place over
the course of 70 days.

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:11.910
The idea of booby traps
in ancient Egyptian tombs

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:14.010
is something that comes
from the imagination

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:17.070
of film writers. None of
the tombs were booby traps.

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:19.980
There were false doors to some tombs,

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:24.390
a corridor designed to lead a
potential thief to a dead end.

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:28.290
There were false corridors,
and there were shafts

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:30.253
to prevent thieves from coming in

00:08:30.253 --> 00:08:33.120
to access the treasure
that was buried in a tomb,

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:35.010
but there were no booby
traps that I'm aware of.

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:36.780
I would give it a minus 1.

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:39.995
It is wholly historically inaccurate.

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:49.170
As they're moving this very heavy statue,

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:51.270
they're asking the woman to pour grease

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:52.740
underneath the statue.

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:56.280
No one knows what methods
they actually used

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:57.795
to move these heavy objects,

00:08:57.795 --> 00:09:01.710
and people take liberties
at guessing how it was done.

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:04.200
Now, there is a famous image in Egypt

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:07.710
where men are moving a statue

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:10.200
and you see a person
standing on top of the statue

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:13.290
and pouring a liquid
in front of the statue,

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:15.843
implying that this liquid is being used

00:09:15.843 --> 00:09:19.860
to ease the movement of
this statue across the sand.

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:22.305
We don't stop a moving
block for an old woman!

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:24.781
Mercy, Prince Moses!

00:09:24.781 --> 00:09:25.680
Come here.

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:29.220
Not for myself, Lord Prince, for a slave.

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:32.610
It's an issue that's been
debated for centuries,

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:34.612
but there is no evidence whatsoever

00:09:34.612 --> 00:09:36.480
of slaves having built the pyramids.

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:40.890
First and foremost, there
are 118 pyramids in Egypt.

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:43.860
And more specifically when
people speak of pyramids,

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:46.380
they're thinking of the
three great pyramids of Giza,

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:49.050
the pyramids of Khufu,
Khafre, and Menkaure.

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:52.920
Those pyramids were all built
during the fourth dynasty,

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:56.070
which is around 2400 BC.

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:58.140
That's at least 1,200 years before

00:09:58.140 --> 00:10:00.120
the story of Moses took place.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:03.270
In recent years, Egyptian
archeologists have actually

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:07.980
found the tombs of the
builders of the pyramids

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:10.260
adjacent to the Giza Plateau.

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:12.810
That honor would not have
been given to a slave.

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:17.340
We know that it was built
by master craftsmen,

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:19.470
master masons, master engineers.

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:22.293
Would you bury the old woman
alive in a tomb of rock?

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:25.920
And there's also one other misnomer

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:27.660
that I think is important to talk about.

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:30.270
The pyramids were not used as tombs.

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:33.330
Pyramids were built over tombs.

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:36.930
I would rate the depiction of the movement

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:39.750
of a large box of stone as accurate.

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:43.278
The idea of someone putting
grease underneath a block

00:10:43.278 --> 00:10:45.750
to help ease this
movement across the sand,

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:47.050
that's not accurate at all.

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:00.510
This story depicts the
battle between Horus and Set,

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:04.200
and it is the second part of the story

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:05.820
of the founding of Egypt.

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:09.750
This is a story that is
over 6,000 years old.

00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:11.066
In this particular instance,

00:11:11.066 --> 00:11:14.857
the animal depiction
of Set is not a jackal,

00:11:14.857 --> 00:11:17.520
it's an unidentified animal.

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:20.107
A jackal was associated with Anubis,

00:11:20.107 --> 00:11:22.620
who was the guardian of the dead.

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:25.590
He was the person who led
the souls into the afterlife.

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:28.530
The depiction of Horus as a falcon

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:31.410
had to do with the symbolism
associated with the falcon.

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:33.360
A falcon has keen eyesight.

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:36.361
Doing Egypt a favor.
You're not fit to be king.

00:11:37.854 --> 00:11:38.880
[laughs]

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:42.300
No, there were not British
white guys in Egypt.

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:43.791
When this picture came out,

00:11:43.791 --> 00:11:45.583
there was a great deal of controversy,

00:11:45.583 --> 00:11:48.735
because there's been a
great deal of contention

00:11:48.735 --> 00:11:51.060
over the last 100 years or so

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:52.770
as to the race of the Egyptians.

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:56.220
And a lot of it unfortunately
has to do with racism,

00:11:56.220 --> 00:12:00.300
because many of the early
founders of Egyptology

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:03.570
happened to be white men who
believed that Black people

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:05.790
were inherently inferior to white.

00:12:05.790 --> 00:12:09.780
The original name for Egypt was Kemet.

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:14.370
Kemet is a word which means
the land of the Blacks.

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:17.670
History has been used as
a tool to subjugate people

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:20.061
and to create false notions

00:12:20.061 --> 00:12:23.253
of superiority and inferiority.

00:12:23.253 --> 00:12:24.660
Thoth, I only need --

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:26.790
The answer to the riddle of the sphinx.

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:28.980
A child could deduce why you're here.

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:32.190
Thoth, the god of wisdom, science,

00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:33.960
astronomy, and knowledge,

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:38.010
who was known in the
original language as Djehuty.

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:39.293
In this particular instance,

00:12:39.293 --> 00:12:43.151
Chad Boseman was quite aware
of ancient Egyptian history,

00:12:43.151 --> 00:12:46.776
and he chose to play this role

00:12:46.776 --> 00:12:49.476
so that the position of Thoth

00:12:49.476 --> 00:12:51.570
could be accurately depicted

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:53.610
by a person of African ancestry.

00:12:53.610 --> 00:12:56.550
I appreciate the fact
that Chad wanted to add

00:12:56.550 --> 00:13:00.120
a sense of historical accuracy
to this particular character.

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:02.340
If I was to rate this
film, the "Gods of Egypt,"

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:04.050
I would give it a 0.

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:07.470
If I were to rate the scenes
with Chadwick Boseman,

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:09.330
I would say give it a 5.

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:11.463
It contains truth, but it is not true.

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:14.575
I will see to that.

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:20.250
The kohl, the dark lining around the eyes,

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:22.260
women wore it and men wore it.

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:24.600
That was used primarily as protection

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:26.160
from the intense sunlight.

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:27.420
It's the same thing that you see

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:31.020
on a football field or a baseball field

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:33.570
where the athlete would put
black underneath their eye

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:36.090
to absorb the intense rays of sunlight

00:13:36.090 --> 00:13:37.640
so that they could see clearly.

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:40.710
In the name of the senate,

00:13:40.710 --> 00:13:43.983
and the people of Rome, and by their will.

00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:49.530
Their representations of the clothing,

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:51.665
the headdresses, is pretty accurate

00:13:51.665 --> 00:13:54.390
based on the physical
evidence that was left.

00:13:54.390 --> 00:13:57.240
The two staffs that we
see Cleopatra holding

00:13:57.240 --> 00:13:59.160
are the crook and the flail.

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:01.140
The crook is the shepherd's crook,

00:14:01.140 --> 00:14:03.570
and it indicates that the king, the ruler,

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:06.240
is a shepherd to the
people of their country.

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:11.057
The flail is an instrument that
is similar to a fly swatter,

00:14:11.057 --> 00:14:15.450
and it's used to swat away
distractions, enemies of Egypt.

00:14:15.450 --> 00:14:17.520
If you combine these two instruments,

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:21.540
then what they represent is
the king or queen's ability

00:14:21.540 --> 00:14:23.640
to protect the people of the land

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:26.580
and to do away with
the enemies of the land

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:30.720
who would bring danger and
threats to the territory.

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:33.750
There cannot be enough hours
in the days of a queen.

00:14:33.750 --> 00:14:37.920
Again, the use of white
actresses to depict Cleopatra

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:40.410
is taking liberties with historical truths

00:14:40.410 --> 00:14:44.490
based on the archeological
evidence that has been found

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:47.400
of Cleopatra's sisters
and other family members.

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:50.550
She was probably closer to Halle Berry

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:52.008
than Elizabeth Taylor.

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:02.222
Now, there's also very little evidence

00:15:02.222 --> 00:15:04.770
to indicate how Cleopatra died.

00:15:04.770 --> 00:15:09.210
So the idea of her holding
an asp to her breast

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:11.580
and it biting her, it's fiction.

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:15.720
There is no evidence to validate
that is how Cleopatra died.

00:15:15.720 --> 00:15:17.340
I give it a 7.

00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:19.380
My favorite of the clips that we've seen

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:21.090
is "Raiders of the Lost Ark."

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:24.773
The narrative was fictional,

00:15:24.773 --> 00:15:28.223
but the presentation of the artifacts

00:15:28.223 --> 00:15:30.390
was as real as can be.

00:15:30.390 --> 00:15:31.590
If you enjoyed this video,

00:15:31.590 --> 00:15:34.233
click the link for more
exciting information.

